# Employee Requirements Tracker — environment configuration
#
# Copy to `.env`, then UNCOMMENT AND FILL only the lines you actually need.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# ── Two things about this file that are not the usual convention ──────────────
#
# 1. Nothing loads it. The application reads System.getenv directly and there is
#    no dotenv library on the classpath, so `.env` is inert until you load it
#    yourself:
#
#        set -a; source .env; set +a; ./kotlin run
#
#    or point your IDE run configuration at it.
#
# 2. Every key is commented out on purpose. An exported-but-empty variable is
#    not the same as an unset one — `System.getenv` returns "" rather than null,
#    and most defaults below are reached by a null check. `DATABASE_URL=` would
#    fail startup in Hikari, and `JWT_ISSUER=` would silently break token
#    verification. Leaving a key commented is what preserves its default.
#
# Everything here has a working default EXCEPT when APP_ENV is set to something
# other than `dev` — see JWT_SECRET and TOKEN_PEPPER.


# ── Runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which deployment this is. Unset or `dev` means development: /openapi, /swagger
# and /metrics are open, and JWT_SECRET and TOKEN_PEPPER may be omitted. Any
# other value puts all three behind HR authentication and makes both secrets
# mandatory.
#
# SET THIS IN EVERY NON-LOCAL DEPLOYMENT. Four controls hang off it, and
# forgetting it does not fail — it silently selects the permissive configuration.
# Expected: dev | staging | production
# APP_ENV=

# TCP port the server binds. Default 8080.
# PORT=


# ── Database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# JDBC URL. Unset means an in-memory H2 in PostgreSQL mode, so a fresh checkout
# and the test suite need no external service. PostgreSQL is the production
# target; Flyway migrates the schema on startup.
# Expected: jdbc:postgresql://<host>:5432/<database>
# DATABASE_URL=

# Role owning the schema. Default `sa`, which suits the H2 fallback.
# DATABASE_USER=

# Default empty, which suits the H2 fallback.
# DATABASE_PASSWORD=

# HikariCP maximum pool size, an integer. Default 10.
# DATABASE_MAX_POOL_SIZE=


# ── HR authentication ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The JWT scheme is a MARKED PLACEHOLDER pending PRD §14 Q4 — who the HR users
# are, whether they share an account, and whether an SSO provider already
# exists. It is to be replaced once Q4 is answered, not extended.

# REQUIRED outside dev: startup refuses rather than boot on a default key. In dev
# an ephemeral key is generated per run, so tokens do not survive a restart.
# Expected: a high-entropy random string, 32+ characters, unique per environment.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# JWT_SECRET=

# Default http://localhost:8080/
# JWT_ISSUER=

# Default employee-requirements-tracker
# JWT_AUDIENCE=

# Default "Employee Requirements Tracker"
# JWT_REALM=


# ── Portal credentials ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Server-side pepper keying the HMAC-SHA-256 digest of upload-link and portal
# session tokens. Keying is what stops a leaked token_hash column from being
# recomputed against a candidate list; determinism is what lets a presented token
# be resolved by its digest at all.
#
# REQUIRED outside dev: startup refuses without it. In dev an ephemeral pepper is
# generated, so links issued before a restart stop resolving.
#
# MUST BE AT LEAST 32 CHARACTERS, in dev too. A short pepper satisfies "is it
# configured" while adding no work to an offline attempt.
#
# ROTATING THIS INVALIDATES EVERY LIVE LINK AND SESSION. There is no credential
# re-issue flow, so rotation currently means re-inviting every in-flight hire by
# hand. Treat it as permanent for the life of an environment.
#
# Expected: a high-entropy random string, distinct from JWT_SECRET.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# TOKEN_PEPPER=


# ── CORS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated hosts allowed to call the API cross-origin. https only, and
# hosts carry no scheme or trailing slash. Unset means no cross-origin host is
# permitted, which is the correct default for a same-origin deployment.
# Expected: hr.example.com,portal.example.com
# CORS_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
